      "text": "I was able to fetch an excerpt from the opening of *Babel*. While the tool was asked to find the first chapter, the returned text appears to be a critical passage from early in the novel, likely setting the stage for Robin Swift's introduction to the English world.\n\nThe excerpt describes the arrival of an Englishman to meet the protagonist:\n\n> \"A man arrived in the afternoon — an Englishman, tall and thin and gleaming with sweat, attended by two Cantonese clerks. He stood in the doorway a long time before he spoke, considering the boy with the detached interest of a man examining a **silver bar at the assay office**.\"\n\nThis opening *immediately* establishes several key elements you mentioned:\n1.  **Narrative Voice:** It uses a close third-person perspective focused on the boy's (Robin's) experience, while maintaining the detached, critical tone of the observing Englishman.\n2.  **Tone:** The tone is immediately one of scrutiny and imbalance. The comparison of the boy to a **\"silver bar at the assay office\"** sharply connects the human subject to the novel's magical/economic currency, which sets a dark, colonial undertone right from the start.\n3.  **Pacing:** The description of the Englishman \"considering the boy... a long time\" slows the pacing down, creating tension before the first lines of dialogue.\n\nThis snippet is a fantastic illustration of how Kuang foreshadows the central conflict!",
